# Governing: SPEC-0002 REQ "Documented Environment Variables".
# Copy this file to .env to override defaults. It ships with defaults only; no secrets.
#
# SERVER RUNTIME VARS (read by the server at process start; changing them needs a restart):
#   Port the Express server listens on.
PORT=4100
# server/src/index.js: production serves the built client; anything else enables CORS for VITE_API_URL origin.
NODE_ENV=development
# Comma-separated origins allowed to call the API when NODE_ENV is not production.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173
# Which peers the server believes `X-Forwarded-*` from (issue #199). UNSET IS THE SAFE
# DEFAULT and is correct for every deployment with nothing in front of the process —
# `docker compose up` publishing 4100 to the host, and the Procfile single-VM run.
#
# Set it ONLY when a reverse proxy really is in front, and prefer naming the proxy over a
# hop count: "loopback" (a proxy on the same host), an address, a CIDR, or a comma-separated
# list of those all compile to a check on the PEER, so a direct client is refused. A bare
# number is a hop count, which express applies without looking at who connected — fine
# behind a managed platform that always terminates for you (Render appends the real client
# IP, so TRUST_PROXY=1 is right there), wrong anywhere the process is reachable directly.
#
# It governs `req.protocol`, `req.ip` and the X-Forwarded-Host lookup — and `req.ip` is what
# both rate limiters key on, so trusting the header on a directly-exposed deploy lets any
# client mint a fresh rate-limit bucket per request. An unparseable value stops the server
# at boot rather than downgrading silently. "true" is treated as unparseable ON PURPOSE:
# express's boolean true trusts every peer at every hop, so the most guessable way to say
# "yes, I have a proxy" is also the most permissive setting there is. Name the proxy.
#
# The other direction, which bites at deploy time: BEHIND A PROXY THAT TERMINATES TLS,
# leaving this unset makes `req.protocol` "http" while the browser sends an https Origin, so
# the same-origin check fails and every write gets a 403. Reads still work (no Origin header
# on a same-origin GET), so it looks like "nothing saves" rather than an outage. Set it in
# the platform's environment BEFORE shipping — see README "Reverse proxies and TRUST_PROXY".
TRUST_PROXY=
# Absolute or relative path to the lowdb store. Unset, the server uses server/data/db.json.
# The test suite points this at a throwaway file so a run cannot touch real data
# (server/src/db.js), and SPEC-0005 names it as the seam the desktop host sets to reach the
# OS per-user data directory — so it stays a documented variable, not a test-only hook.
OUTFITTER_DB_FILE=
#
# CLIENT BUILD-TIME VAR (inlined by Vite when `npm run build -w client` runs; a change
# requires a client REBUILD, not just a server restart):
# Base URL the client uses for API calls.
VITE_API_URL=/api
